/Title (Acne vulgaris is one of the most prevalent dermatological conditions worldwide, affecting a significant portion of the population in both adolescence and adulthood. Acne rates have been rising since the 1990s, with Western Europe identified as having the highest prevalence among world regions [1]. High-income, industrialised regions \(including Europe and North America\) report the greatest acne burden, likely due to lifestyle factors such as diet and urban environment [2]. The United States, for example, is estimated to have about 50 million people affected by acne at any given time.[2])
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 325 0 R
/Next 576 0 R
>>
endobj
450 0 obj
<<
/Title (The takeaway for Research and Development \(R&D\) professionals is that acne is not merely a teenage rite of passage; it is a widespread, chronic condition across age groups and geographies, with an increasing population of adult sufferers in Europe and North America. This shifting epidemiology drives a strong demand for new acne solutions that cater to adults as well as adolescents [2].)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 325 0 R
/Prev 577 0 R
>>
endobj
